 ## Overview
-Memory Layout Optimization encompasses techniques for arranging volumetric data in linear memory to maximize bandwidth utilization, minimize cache misses, and eliminate hardware-level memory bank conflicts during ray casting [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that standard row-major or column-major layouts suffer from poor spatial locality when rays traverse 3D volumes along arbitrary directions, leading to inefficient memory access patterns [S1, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the focus on low-level data placement (bit interleaving, bank skewing, block packing) rather than high-level traversal algorithms or indexing structures; it builds on [Dense grids and memory layout](3-dense-grids-and-memory-layout.md) by defining exactly how the dense grid is laid out in bytes.
+Memory Layout Optimization encompasses techniques for arranging volumetric data in linear memory to maximize bandwidth utilization, minimize cache misses, and eliminate hardware-level memory bank conflicts during ray casting [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that standard row-major or column-major layouts suffer from poor spatial locality when rays traverse 3D volumes along arbitrary directions, leading to inefficient memory access patterns [G]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the focus on low-level data placement (bit interleaving, bank skewing, block packing) rather than high-level traversal algorithms or indexing structures; it builds on [Dense grids and memory layout](3-dense-grids-and-memory-layout.md) by defining exactly how the dense grid is laid out in bytes [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family map 3D spatial coordinates $(u, v, w)$ to a linear memory address or buffer index to improve spatial locality. The core mechanism involves decomposing the volume into small, fixed-size blocks (miniblocks, chunks, or tiles) and applying a transformation to the coordinate bits or block indices. Common mathematical operations include:
-1.  **Integer Division:** Mapping voxel coordinates to block/miniblock coordinates using floor division (e.g., $u_m = \lfloor u / 2 \rfloor$) [S1, unofficial].
-2.  **Modulo Arithmetic:** Distributing blocks across memory modules or banks using modulo operations (e.g., $M = (u_m + v_m + w_m) \bmod 4$) to ensure adjacent blocks map to distinct hardware resources [S1, unofficial].
-3.  **Bit Interleaving:** Rearranging the bits of coordinate components to group spatially adjacent voxels in memory, often separating most significant bits (MSBs) from least significant bits (LSBs) [S1, unofficial].
-4.  **Padding/Alignment:** Padding volume dimensions to multiples of specific block sizes (e.g., 32) to ensure uniform access patterns and simplify hardware logic [S1, unofficial].
